<Project>
  <!--
    Workaround: The Windows App SDK's MrtCore.PriGen.targets unconditionally loads
    Microsoft.Build.Packaging.Pri.Tasks.dll and Microsoft.Build.AppxPackage.dll from
    AppxMSBuildToolsPath, which only exists inside a full Visual Studio installation.

    For unpackaged WinUI 3 apps built from the dotnet CLI, we redirect
    AppxMSBuildToolsPath to no-op stub assemblies in build/stubs/.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AppxPackage>false</AppxPackage>
    <AppxMSBuildToolsPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\stubs\</AppxMSBuildToolsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    VAD-disable response lever. Committed default TRUE — the Silero VAD
    no-speech gate is on in every ordinary build, and it stays that way.

    An incident build opts OUT with `-p:VadEnabled=false`, which defines the
    VAD_DISABLED symbol; VadFeature.IsEnabled then flips and BOTH VAD consumers
    short-circuit without native contact:

      - VoiceActivityDetectionService returns NoSpeechVerdict.Unavailable,
        degrading to the legacy whole-file RMS gate.
      - SherpaVadSegmenter returns Disabled, so VadDecodePlan falls back to
        LongAudioChunker's fixed-window plan (2026-08-20).

    That is a downgrade accepted only to stop a hard process kill: it reinstates
    the silence-hallucination defect PR #236 fixed. (The VAD-segmented decode
    DEFAULT was rejected by measurement before shipping — 2026-08-21 — so the
    second consumer, SherpaVadSegmenter, currently has no app caller; the lever
    still gates it so any future wiring inherits the contract.) A disabled build
    always still transcribes. Pull it ONLY for a confirmed vcomp140/OpenMP access
    violation (0xc0000005) on a mitigated build, or a soak-quota shortfall at
    go/no-go; never for an unexplained crash, a slow VAD, or a false no-speech
    block, which have their own diagnoses. Two proofs, and the FILE is the one to
    trust: vad-disabled.marker at the publish root is there regardless of what the
    user does, while the runtime pair — a "VAD disabled by build" line and no
    "Silero VAD initialized:" line — only exists once a recording has run.

    The second consumer is gated deliberately. The crash this lever answers was
    observed in whisper.cpp's ggml/OpenMP path and not in sherpa's, but its promise
    is "no VAD native code" — a second implementation that ignored it would make
    the runbook a lie.

    Note the polarity is the INVERSE of UpdateCheckEnabled (which ships false and
    release builds opt in), so the symbol names the non-default state and an
    ordinary build defines nothing.

    Lives here rather than in VoiceWink.csproj because the test project needs the
    same symbol to compile its configuration-scoped assertions
    (VadFeatureGateTests); a property two projects consume belongs in the shared
    props file rather than being copy-pasted into both, where it could drift.

    Values other than 'true'/'false' are REJECTED by ValidateVadEnabled in
    Directory.Build.targets — a typo must never silently produce a VAD-ENABLED
    build that the operator believes is disabled.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VadEnabled Condition="'$(VadEnabled)' == ''">true</VadEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(VadEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);VAD_DISABLED</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The parakeet.cpp server-backend rollout flag. Committed default TRUE since the engine
    rollout flip (2026-08-24) - every ordinary build compiles the
    parakeet.cpp-backed path and PcppFeature.IsEnabled is const true. `-p:PcppEnabled=false`
    is the KILL SWITCH's build form: it restores the sherpa-only era including the sherpa
    catalog row (ParakeetCatalog.ActiveRow follows this flag). The OFF configuration has NO
    automated executor - its weekly lever-check leg was owner-retired the day it shipped
    (2026-08-24, "we are not going back to the old engine"), so an incident pull of this
    switch should expect to fix the rebuild first. The symbol still
    names the PCPP_ENABLED state (opposite polarity to the disable levers above, deliberately:
    when this flag was introduced the non-default was the NEW backend, and renaming the symbol
    at the flip would touch every gate for zero information). Values other than 'true'/'false'
    are REJECTED by ValidatePcppEnabled in Directory.Build.targets, same as the VAD lever.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PcppEnabled Condition="'$(PcppEnabled)' == ''">true</PcppEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PcppEnabled)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);PCPP_ENABLED</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The capture path's anti-alias low-pass. Committed default TRUE.

    `-p:AntiAliasEnabled=false` defines ANTIALIAS_DISABLED; CaptureFilterFeature.IsEnabled
    flips and AudioRecorderService takes the legacy resample path verbatim
    (AudioResampler.ResampleInto, per-buffer, unfiltered). Same polarity as
    VadEnabled — the symbol names the non-default state.

    SCOPE IS NARROWER THAN THE VAD LEVER ON PURPOSE, and that is not an oversight:
    this is a build-time A/B and diagnostic lever, with no release.yml input, no
    release-update.ps1 switch, no publish marker and no runbook. The VAD lever has
    all of those because it guards an unroot-caused NATIVE crash where shipping
    the disabled build IS the mitigation. This is managed DSP with unit coverage;
    its incident response is reverting the commit. Full argument in
    Helpers/CaptureFilterFeature.cs.

    Lives here rather than in VoiceWink.csproj for the same reason as VadEnabled:
    the test project needs the symbol to compile CaptureFilterFeatureGateTests.

    Values other than 'true'/'false' are REJECTED by ValidateAntiAliasEnabled in
    Directory.Build.targets.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AntiAliasEnabled Condition="'$(AntiAliasEnabled)' == ''">true</AntiAliasEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(AntiAliasEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);ANTIALIAS_DISABLED</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The same lever for the SECOND native runtime (sherpa-onnx).
    Committed default TRUE.

    An incident build opts out with `-p:ParakeetEnabled=false`, defining
    PARAKEET_DISABLED; ParakeetFeature.IsEnabled flips, and the runtime reports
    PrepareOutcome.Unavailable rather than loading any native code. Unlike the VAD
    lever this is not a downgrade of a shipped guarantee — it removes an OPTIONAL
    engine, and Whisper keeps serving every model it already served.

    Exists because the VAD crash is the standing lesson that a native dependency working
    on every dev machine can still fault on a user's, and a second one doubles that
    surface. Same polarity as VadEnabled: the symbol names the non-default state,
    so an ordinary build defines nothing.

    Values other than 'true'/'false' are REJECTED by ValidateParakeetEnabled in
    Directory.Build.targets — a typo must never silently produce an ENABLED build
    the operator believes is disabled.

    Pull it for crashes attributable to onnxruntime.dll / sherpa-onnx-c-api.dll, or
    to the Parakeet recording path; never for a wrong or empty transcription, which
    is a model-quality question and would take the engine from every user who chose
    it. Verify a lever build by parakeet-disabled.marker at the publish root
    (content = version) — present regardless of what the user does, where the
    runtime log line appears only once someone tries to prepare a Parakeet model.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ParakeetEnabled Condition="'$(ParakeetEnabled)' == ''">true</ParakeetEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ParakeetEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);PARAKEET_DISABLED</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
